WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:43.610
- Hi everyone,

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.690
welcome to Khan Academy's
daily homeroom livestream.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.620
For those of you all who
do not know what this is,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.440
this is something that we thought of

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.420
when we started seeing
mass school closures

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.990
and we know that people
are gonna be at home,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:55.980
socially distanced.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.735
And obviously Khan Academy,
we saw ourselves as,

00:00:58.735 --> 00:01:01.090
it's our duty to step up
in this time of closure.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.680
So a lot of our resources
are the way that many

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.600
of y'all might be able to keep learning

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.310
or have your children or
your students keep learning.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.140
But we've also trying
to do other supports.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.580
We thought it'd be just fun
to have a synchronous way

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.360
to have interesting
conversations between ourselves

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:17.707
to bring on interesting guests

00:01:17.707 --> 00:01:21.006
from all over the world
on all sorts of topics.

00:01:21.006 --> 00:01:23.740
I am doing an interesting experiment today

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.900
that we should all have a
growth mindset about things.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.690
I have my phone leaned against
my computer right over here,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.170
so that I can simultaneously
do this on Instagram.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.992
So normally we have some
technology that Philippe has found

00:01:34.992 --> 00:01:37.290
(laughs) that allows us
to do this on Facebook

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.700
and YouTube simultaneously, but
now we have added Instagram,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.730
so I'm saying hi to all of the
folks on Instagram as well.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.780
So today's topic is going
to be really interesting.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.360
It's going to be
graduation and life advice,

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.930
and you can almost view this is like

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.780
interactive graduation address,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.200
which is frankly better than
just me sitting at a podium

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.360
and giving you something
that you might not remember

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.130
for the next 20 minutes.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.210
But before we break into the Q&amp;A,

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.115
start thinking of those
life advice questions

00:02:03.115 --> 00:02:05.950
that you would like, and
we have team members,

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.360
I'm gonna be looking at the Instagram

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.420
and then our team members are
going to be helping me out

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.120
with YouTube and Facebook.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.213
I do want to give a special
thanks to several corporations

00:02:14.213 --> 00:02:16.940
that have stepped up over
the last several weeks,

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.382
especially with the COVID crisis.

00:02:18.382 --> 00:02:20.250
Khan Academy is a not-for-profit.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.800
We can only exist because
of philanthropic donations

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.691
from folks like yourself,
and from corporations

00:02:25.691 --> 00:02:30.412
and foundations, and folks like
Bank of America, Google.org,

00:02:30.412 --> 00:02:31.480
AT&amp;T.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.010
Fastly and Novartis have stepped up

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.330
just in the last few months

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:35.985
when they realized that our
costs were going even higher,

00:02:35.985 --> 00:02:40.020
(laughs) because our
usage, our server costs,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.440
our usage is about two and a half,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.780
three X of what it typically is.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.030
We're trying to accelerate
all programs forward.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.430
So thank you to all of them.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.400
And once again, if you're
in a position to donate,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.430
every dollar matters,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.190
it helps us to show sure of confidence.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.010
It helps us reach that many more folks

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.750
who otherwise would not
be able to have access

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:59.025
to some form of a
free-world class education,

00:02:59.025 --> 00:03:02.280
or teachers who would not have
access to tools like this.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.260
So with that,

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.220
I would love to take any
questions that you have.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.799
And I already see a couple of questions.

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:10.460
Oh, actually it looks like my team

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:11.690
is also scanning Instagram,

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.320
so this is super helpful for me.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.320
So from Instagram, at
SRG underscore 19 asks,

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.683
what is your favorite
memory from high school?

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:25.970
You're taking me back.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.520
All right, let me see.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.360
I don't know if it's my favorite memory,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.193
but I could probably
think of some memories.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.540
I remember there was a day
I was in student council

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.300
and we were, in student
council in high school

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.950
you paint those pep rally
type banners and stuff,

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.330
and there was, someone had
forgot to close the yellow paint,

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.930
and I wanted to make a point that like,

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.330
when you don't close the yellow paint,

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.010
the yellow paint all dries up.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.580
And so I was like,
"Look, this is all dry."

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.780
And I did this and I was
like, "Look how dry it is."

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.480
And then it wasn't dry.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.240
And so the yellow paint,
that was a good memory.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.576
It helped me to take myself
a little less seriously,

00:04:04.576 --> 00:04:06.924
(laughs) because the
universe was conspiring

00:04:06.924 --> 00:04:09.873
to keep me from being too self-righteous.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.610
Let's see other great
memories from high school.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:14.210
I mean there's great memories.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:17.143
I remember we went on a
camping trip, my senior year.

00:04:17.143 --> 00:04:18.650
That was great, and we got lost.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:19.700
So it's always those memories

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.350
where it's a little bit of
an adventure, that's nice.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.260
There's some things I cringe at,

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.313
some of you all may or may not know that,

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.470
I did give the graduation address

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.530
at my high school graduation

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.420
and I was a little bit angsty.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:40.420
I was also, by my senior
year, I was not in a band,

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:44.030
but my sophomore year I was a lead singer

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:44.870
in heavy metal band.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.420
And so I had that little angsty in me.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.276
And so at graduation, I felt the need,

00:04:51.276 --> 00:04:53.240
and I cringe a little bit about it now,

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:57.210
but I guess it is a memory
to make a statement.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:01.800
So the morning of graduation,
my friend and myself,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.450
we actually snuck into the auditorium

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.760
where the graduation is going to happen

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.980
and we put a guitar
under the diploma table.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.610
And so when I went up to give a speech,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.460
I said, "I think it would
be better if I sang this."

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:18.690
And so I took the guitar out

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.670
from underneath the diploma table.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.890
I still remember the look
on my principal's face,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:26.500
it was just like horror (laughs)

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.943
and frankly half the crowd, and I sang.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.050
So, I cringe on it now.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.370
I wouldn't do that today, and
I wouldn't recommend it today.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.220
But it was it was novel.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.771
So I'll try to think of
more and more memories

00:05:42.771 --> 00:05:45.570
as we go through this session.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.690
But that's a good question.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:51.300
So from Instagram, @leanmarvelous asks,

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.900
how old were you when you realized

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.160
that helping others to learn
was a passion of yours?

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.763
Great question, Lean Marvelous.

00:06:00.491 --> 00:06:01.773
And if you see me looking back and forth,

00:06:01.773 --> 00:06:03.370
I'm looking at my computer screen

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:04.690
and I'm looking at the Instagram screen

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:08.119
so that I'm making eye
contact with everyone.

00:06:08.119 --> 00:06:13.119
So I realized I really
enjoyed tutoring folks.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.110
It was actually around that
same time in high school,

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:18.770
and actually that even jog some
other really great memories

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:21.410
but it might not surprise
folks that I was the captain

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.229
or the president of the math club.

00:06:23.229 --> 00:06:24.211
(chuckles)

00:06:24.211 --> 00:06:25.130
That is cool.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:26.410
It was called new alpha theater.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.610
I don't know if that's a thing
where you go to high school.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.630
But as part of it, we
wanted to do service,

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:35.190
and we figured a valuable service

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.350
would be to tutor our peers.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.240
And so we used to do this
afterschool tutoring thing

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.460
for our peers and a lot of
kids actually showed up for it.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.960
And it was then that I realized
that if you give students,

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.400
if you give anyone one-on-one attention,

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.640
give them a chance to fill in the gaps,

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.670
that they're really capable
of learning almost anything.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.860
And I saw it before my
eyes, a lot of my friends,

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:02.050
or even people I didn't
know who were C students

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.580
who thought they weren't good at math

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.170
when they just got a
little bit of attention,

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.890
they were able to catch up

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.130
and do whatever they needed to do.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.380
So that was the first time I
realized I really enjoyed it.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:14.850
Then when I was in college,

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.010
I did a little bit of that as well.

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:21.110
There was a local school, had budget cuts.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.370
And so they needed some
more, I guess you could say,

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.180
volunteer teachers or teacher assistance.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:27.630
And so myself and my roommate,
we went and volunteered.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.300
This was in Brookline,
Massachusetts, and really enjoyed it.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.547
I realized that I had no
classroom management skills.

00:07:32.547 --> 00:07:33.380
(laughs)

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:38.380
A lot of your teachers can
appreciate how important that is,

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:39.868
I didn't at the time.

00:07:39.868 --> 00:07:42.520
There were moments where
I had some fifth graders

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.670
standing on top of a table

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:45.570
and I had to spend a little
bit more time than I'd like

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:46.460
telling them to get off.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.310
But I realized that that
was intriguing to me.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:51.270
And I think it was after my junior year,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:55.120
I got a fellowship to work on software.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:57.460
I had applied for it, work
on educational software

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:59.670
for students with
attention deficit disorder.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.850
It was called an aeleron to
fellowship at my undergrad.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:04.515
And that was the first time I
started writing some software

00:08:04.515 --> 00:08:08.263
and seeing if this could
be of help to folks.

00:08:09.518 --> 00:08:13.850
And then the life took
over, pragmatism took over

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.380
and graduating from college,

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.307
I had a a reasonable
amount of debt and I said,

00:08:19.307 --> 00:08:20.360
"I need to make some money,

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.850
maybe help out the family a little bit."

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:25.390
So I went into tack, which
I also enjoy my background.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:28.720
My undergraduate degrees were
in math and computer science.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:31.600
And many of you all know the story.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:32.970
I then go to business school,

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:34.320
I become a hedge fund analyst,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.960
but then while that was
happening, my cousins needed help.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.530
And so it kindled that same
joy for the subject matter

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:44.980
and for being able to help
folks especially family members.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:46.840
And so that's what got me on the track

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.050
and the more I did it,
the more that I saw gains

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:51.170
in my cousins and other folks that I knew.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:52.790
And frankly, the more that I got letters

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.244
from random people on the Internet

00:08:54.244 --> 00:08:56.194
(chuckles) saying that it helping them.

00:08:57.124 --> 00:08:59.130
I don't think there's
anything special about me.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.330
I think anyone would find
that incredibly rewarding,

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:02.880
and it was a little bit addictive,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.080
you start to realize how
much more joyful that is

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:07.910
when you get a letter
from someone in Indonesia

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:11.531
saying that you helped them
get back to going to college

00:09:11.531 --> 00:09:14.020
versus you get a slightly larger bonus

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.243
at your hedge fund job,

00:09:16.243 --> 00:09:17.492
and you realize that

00:09:17.492 --> 00:09:20.040
maybe this is what you
could do for a living.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.420
So it was just, I guess
you could say signs

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.210
that were showing up
in my life throughout.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.150
And at some point you
just have to pay attention

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.350
to those signs (laughs)

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.920
they'd keep trying to make themselves

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.283
a little bit more visible.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:33.560
So let's see, from Instagram,

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:34.880
a lot of questions from Instagram,

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.530
we should do this more regularly,

00:09:37.416 --> 00:09:40.750
have a livestream all of the above.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:43.857
Andy Chavez, Oh, it disappeared, said,

00:09:43.857 --> 00:09:48.027
"How do you learn from failure ..."

00:09:48.027 --> 00:09:48.987
Oh, there it goes.

00:09:48.987 --> 00:09:50.560
"How do you learn from failure?"

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.560
So, I think the first
step is to just recognize

00:09:55.970 --> 00:10:00.050
that the failure is not a,
it's not a judgment on you.

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:04.260
I think so many times we confuse ourselves

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:08.110
with our thoughts, with our labels,

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:10.600
that society is putting
on and things like grades,

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.070
or you failed, or you didn't
... That's kind of a label

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:14.930
or some extrinsic thing
that happens to you

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.560
and to realize that's not yourself,

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.740
and to realize that those
are the moments of growth.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.470
The very fact that you're
failing is a good sign.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:23.840
It's a sign that you're pushing yourself

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:24.740
out of your comfort zone,

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.950
you're not just resting on your laurels.

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:30.150
And and if you reflect on that failure,

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:32.150
and I recently met a teacher

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.540
who likes to use the phrase "fail forward"

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.157
So if you reflect on
that failure and you say,

00:10:36.157 --> 00:10:37.640
"Okay, what can I learn from it

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:39.560
so that maybe next time I try,

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:41.720
I have a little bit lower
chance of at least failing

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:42.600
in that way."

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.420
Then I'm making progress.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:45.926
That's how you grow as a human being.

00:10:45.926 --> 00:10:50.040
And this mystery that we all
live, (chuckles) whole life,

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.513
and it is really a mystery,

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:57.680
all I can think of the best
thing to do is to keep on going,

00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:59.120
keep on growing, keep on failing

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:00.330
and keep on learning from that.

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:02.900
And when you do that, and
actually when you learn,

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:04.800
when you see that you can
get onto the other side

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.420
of failure, even once, I think it was a,

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.352
we had Alberta Carvallo, who's
superintendent of Miami Dade,

00:11:10.352 --> 00:11:12.060
he said something about that.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:14.070
The first time that you do
something a little bit scary

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:15.240
and you even fail a little bit,

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:16.300
but then you power through it

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:18.512
and you realize that you can
get to the other side of it

00:11:18.512 --> 00:11:21.260
that's when you realize that's a muscle.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.010
And then next time when
you see an obstacle,

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:23.847
when you see the failure you're like,

00:11:23.847 --> 00:11:25.940
"Well, it's just gonna
be like last time, sure,

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.554
I might try a little bit,
I might fail a little bit,

00:11:28.554 --> 00:11:31.080
but I just got to keep
chipping away at that thing,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:34.980
and before I know it, I'm gonna
be able to get through it."

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:36.540
So that's the best advice.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.121
And actually there's a
ton of research on that,

00:11:38.121 --> 00:11:41.510
that those who are willing
to embrace failure,

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:43.490
those were willing to step
out of their comfort zone

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:46.050
and learn from it, who defined themselves

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.960
not by the outcome by success or failure,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.747
but who defined themselves by,

00:11:50.747 --> 00:11:52.830
"Hey, I'm someone who's
always willing to try,

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:56.310
I'm someone who's always
willing to do the hard thing."

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:58.299
Those are the people who
do really well in life

00:11:58.299 --> 00:12:02.803
without fail, (laughs)
not to overuse the word.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:09.660
So let's see, from
Instagram, it's me Shreash,

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:12.200
asks, how was your graduation day?

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:16.170
So actually I've had
several graduation days,

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.290
high school and then undergrad

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.710
and then from business school,

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:23.580
I would say I've already described
my high school graduation

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:24.413
a little bit.

00:12:24.413 --> 00:12:26.180
I have to say graduation days

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:28.550
have been unusually eventful for me.

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:30.593
So when I was in college,

00:12:31.552 --> 00:12:33.700
I was my senior class president at MIT,

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:37.170
so I was on the commencement committee.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:39.380
And at the time the President
of the United States

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:40.820
was Bill Clinton.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.930
So you can imagine when we
used to have our meetings

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:43.940
for the commencement committee,

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:45.960
this was in November of my senior year,

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:49.747
this was in 1997, we
had these meetings like,

00:12:49.747 --> 00:12:51.386
"Who should we invite for commencement?"

00:12:51.386 --> 00:12:54.026
And the previous year Al Gore had spoken,

00:12:54.026 --> 00:12:55.903
and so I was just like,

00:12:55.903 --> 00:12:58.693
" Why don't we invite the
president of the United States?"

00:12:58.693 --> 00:12:59.526
(laughs)

00:12:59.526 --> 00:13:00.790
I was just thinking, why not?

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:02.993
See if the president will show up.

00:13:02.993 --> 00:13:06.390
And the Vice President
showed up a year before.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:09.160
And I remember President Vest,

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.107
who was the President of
MIT at the time, said,

00:13:11.107 --> 00:13:12.300
"Sal, first of all,

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:13.920
there's no way the
president's gonna to show up.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:16.572
Second of all, it's a bit
of a pain with the security

00:13:16.572 --> 00:13:18.130
chuckles) and all logistics."

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.680
And even though the Vice
PResident was pretty tough,

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:21.990
the president would be even harder.

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:23.426
So anyway, I'm like, "All right."

00:13:23.426 --> 00:13:27.550
So we actually did invite, that
year's time man of the year

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.810
was someone by the name of David Ho,

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:32.980
who helped develop some of
the antivirals that helped

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:35.540
kind of cure AIDS.

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:37.700
So big deal time man of the year

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:38.710
was our commencement speaker.

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:40.520
I was excited about that.

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:43.300
And so you fast forward to about,

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:47.120
I think it was like three
days before commencement,

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.034
and I get an email from
President Vest saying,

00:13:51.034 --> 00:13:53.260
"Sal, are you okay with this?"

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.800
And it was a forward from the White House.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:59.230
And it said, "President Clinton

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:03.340
would like to give a
major speech or whatever

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:07.240
on the digital divide
at MIT's commencement.

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.800
And he would he's
inviting himself (laughs)

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:11.780
to be the commencement speaker."

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.857
And so I just responded back, I was like,

00:14:13.857 --> 00:14:15.560
"Yeah, I'm cool with this."

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:18.630
And so (chuckles) he ended up
being our commencement speaker

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:22.080
with a few days notice,
and then it was literally,

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.560
Oh, no, it was more than that,
it was now that I remember,

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:27.850
that email was actually
probably about a month before

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.010
commencement because obviously
all of the logistics,

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.970
and then the night before
commencement was actually

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:35.957
when I got the other email
from President Vest saying,

00:14:35.957 --> 00:14:37.470
"Are you okay with this?"

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:42.431
And it said that "President
Clinton has some time

00:14:42.431 --> 00:14:44.270
and might be coming an hour early

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.430
and would like to meet
with a few student leaders"

00:14:46.430 --> 00:14:48.550
And so this was just one
of these random things

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:51.600
where it was me and six
other student leaders,

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.070
we got to spend an an hour

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.820
with the President of United States

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:57.643
right before our graduation.

00:14:57.643 --> 00:14:59.559
Regardless of your political affiliation,

00:14:59.559 --> 00:15:02.120
regardless of anything,

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.499
it's a pretty heady experience

00:15:03.499 --> 00:15:07.250
if you're 21 years old to graduation day

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:10.340
and the President United States
wants to hang out with you

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:12.010
before graduation for an hour.

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:14.670
And it really was kind
of like hanging out,

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:16.310
it was really weird.

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:17.640
I ended a good way.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:21.595
'Cause you normally think
these people are some,

00:15:21.595 --> 00:15:23.860
you don't realize there are people too.

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:25.690
So it was a fascinating conversation.

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:29.030
And then right when we were
all getting out of the door,

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:32.437
ready to go line up for
commencement President Clinton says,

00:15:32.437 --> 00:15:34.620
"I think I have a little
bit of time after my speech.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:35.940
I would like to give

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.260
a couple of student
leaders their diplomas."

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:43.260
And so I gave my little two minutes speech

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.630
as class president, then President Clinton

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:46.970
gave his digital divide speech,

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:48.010
then David Ho gave his speech.

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:50.200
So we had two commencement speakers,

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:52.540
and then President Clinton
gave me and three other people,

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:54.660
our diplomas, and then he got
on his helicopter and left.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:57.930
So that was ... (chuckles)

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:01.125
I was like, "Wow, how did that happen?"

00:16:01.125 --> 00:16:04.620
It was definitely a
powerful experience for me.

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:07.140
And once again, regardless
of where you are

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:10.275
on the political spectrum,
regardless of anything,

00:16:10.275 --> 00:16:11.330
when you're young

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:14.930
and you realize that there's
these people who have done,

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.780
who are very influential in the world

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:19.650
that they are still human beings

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.360
and that you can connect
with them and talk to them.

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:24.870
It did open my operatory,
it did make me think,

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:27.707
okay, wow, maybe this is a world

00:16:27.707 --> 00:16:30.970
in which I too might have
something to contribute.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:32.710
So you can't discount
something like that."

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:37.400
So that was a very powerful experience.

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:42.400
So let's see, Instagram, Kayondo.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:46.200
How did you feel right after
graduating from college?

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.003
So this is an interesting question.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:53.020
I actually, there's a
little bit of excitement

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:54.410
that you're graduating from college,

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:58.339
that all those problem sets
and deadlines (chuckles)

00:16:58.339 --> 00:17:02.060
and all of those things that
college academic rat race

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:04.470
is a little bit over, but
I have to, in hindsight,

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:09.470
I realized that it was sad
because so much of your identity,

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.660
your friendships, they're all at college.

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:14.080
And I was excited I was going
to move out to California

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:15.355
for a job, my first job was gonna be

00:17:15.355 --> 00:17:17.374
a product manager at Oracle.

00:17:17.374 --> 00:17:19.780
And I was excited about that,

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:22.640
but I didn't know anyone
out in Northern California.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.972
And so when I came out here,
I have to admit, it was tough.

00:17:25.972 --> 00:17:29.480
I tell a lot of folks that
first year out of college

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.880
going to a place where
I didn't know anyone,

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:34.760
I didn't have any really good friends,

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:36.090
I spent a lot of nights (chuckles)

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:38.276
just hanging out at the
Barnes and Nobles (chuckles)

00:17:38.276 --> 00:17:39.521
reading books for free.

00:17:39.521 --> 00:17:40.354
(laughs)

00:17:40.354 --> 00:17:42.132
That was my social life.

00:17:42.132 --> 00:17:45.863
It wasn't the best year for
me, it was a tough year.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:48.550
And then slowly ...

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:50.310
What happened was I had a lot of friends

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:51.560
who we were with,

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:53.650
and I finished my degree
a little bit early,

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:55.290
so I came out a year earlier.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:58.320
And so when more of my friends
came out the next year,

00:17:58.320 --> 00:17:59.660
then it started to get
a little bit better.

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:01.670
But that first year out of college,

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:03.630
I think it can be a little bit jarring,

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:06.980
'cause I was fortunate, I had a good job,

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:09.340
I had a good boss, I had good coworkers,

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:11.930
but it's still not the
same thing as what you get

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:15.980
when you're in the dorm
room pontificating things,

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:18.550
doing silly things that you do in college.

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:20.360
Still when I get together
with my college friends,

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:23.507
we reminisce about a lot
of the silliness (chuckles)

00:18:23.507 --> 00:18:26.284
that happened in college.

00:18:26.284 --> 00:18:29.390
So yeah, it was tough,

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:30.950
but then slowly but surely

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.590
it got better as more and more friends,

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:35.070
and I met new people,

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:38.110
and it is all about keeping
an open mind to things,

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:39.080
to new people

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:43.385
and to not just grieving
for what you might've lost.

00:18:43.385 --> 00:18:46.540
And then, but I was eager to
go back to business school

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:47.920
because business school
had a little bit of

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:49.133
that element as well.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:52.710
Just the sense of community,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:54.930
And then after business
school, by that point,

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:57.283
I had a fiance, so I
was feeling less lonely.

00:18:57.283 --> 00:18:58.170
(laughs)

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:03.170
So I post business school
life felt pretty good.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.773
And then she married me.

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:11.957
So Instagram, Aiden Jaffa Rover says,

00:19:11.957 --> 00:19:13.750
"What do you wish you would have known

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:16.027
when you graduated from high school?"

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:22.540
I think, I was a reasonably,

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:25.260
I'm fundamentally an introverted person.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:27.772
Like if I go to a big
dinner party I'll enjoy it,

00:19:27.772 --> 00:19:31.180
but then I like to come
home and just decompress

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:32.700
and have quiet time for myself,

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.900
that's when I re-energize.

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:36.850
And even when I went to college

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:38.810
and actually in high school as well,

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:40.630
I always did have the mindset that,

00:19:40.630 --> 00:19:43.350
Hey, I wanna step out of my comfort zone,

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:44.940
I wanna try new things,

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:49.940
I wanna try to meet new
people and be open to things.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:51.611
But I think especially in high school

00:19:51.611 --> 00:19:53.160
and to a lesser degree in college,

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:54.140
but especially in high school,

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:55.697
it's very easy to get
into your head and saying,

00:19:55.697 --> 00:19:59.710
"Oh, well, they probably don't really,

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:01.410
wouldn't be interested in hanging out

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:05.700
or they're in that
crowd, I'm in this crowd,

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:06.790
we're friendly,

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:11.560
but we really wouldn't get
a lot from each other."

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:13.120
And I think that's a mistake.

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.770
I think you actually have the chance

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:18.630
to get the most from the
folks in your high school

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:21.433
and in your college that
are different from you.

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:24.780
I now realize in hindsight,

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:28.050
and I've met many of these
people now that we're adults,

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:31.920
and everyone has those same
insecurities, everyone.

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:33.080
I don't care who it is.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:36.600
It could be the the captain
of the football team,

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:38.740
the homecoming King, Queen.

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:40.900
Everyone's oftentimes some
of them will have more

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:42.457
of those insecurities
because they're afraid

00:20:42.457 --> 00:20:46.280
their identity is so defined
by some of these things,

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:47.390
these external things.

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:50.450
And so I think if you're an open person

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:52.853
who's genuinely interested
in other people,

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:57.120
you can form deeper
connections with anyone

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:58.170
and they will appreciate it.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:00.680
And even on first cut,

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:02.280
they might be even a
little bit weirded out.

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:06.059
I mean, you don't do
things that are truly scary

00:21:06.059 --> 00:21:08.097
(chuckles) but if you're like,
"Hey, do you need anything?"

00:21:08.097 --> 00:21:09.637
"Hey, I wanna help you."

00:21:09.637 --> 00:21:11.787
"Hey, tell me about your family."

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:14.030
At first, you're like,
"Why are people asking?

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:16.200
is he interested in me?"

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.230
But then people are like,
"Oh, well, you wanna know?

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:19.063
I'll tell you."

00:21:19.063 --> 00:21:22.130
And I think those deep
connections are super powerful.

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:24.407
I think another thing
that I wish I knew better

00:21:24.407 --> 00:21:28.620
in high school is that, it's
easy to treat your teachers

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:32.433
as teachers and only teachers.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:35.749
And I did form, I would say a friendship

00:21:35.749 --> 00:21:40.010
with a handful of teachers,
Mr. Hernandez, Miss Kennedy,

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:42.000
are the ones in high school
that definitely come to mind

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.430
that I could talk to about things.

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:46.990
And they would talk to me about stuff.

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:49.550
It was really powerful.

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:51.460
They treated me more like a peer,

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:53.710
and obviously sometimes
they had to play teacher

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:55.870
and I was a student, but a lot of times

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:59.700
there was a nice comradery between us.

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:03.607
But I think for other times
it was very easy to say,

00:22:03.607 --> 00:22:06.561
"Oh, so and so, that teacher is like this.

00:22:06.561 --> 00:22:09.537
They like to fail students
and they're like that."

00:22:09.537 --> 00:22:12.460
And in hindsight, I
realized how wrong it is

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:16.780
because 99.99999% of
the teachers that I had,

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:18.400
and that I'm sure you have,

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:20.810
their number one priority
was to try to prepare you

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:21.643
for the world,

00:22:21.643 --> 00:22:22.960
and make you a better human being.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:27.470
And I wish, I told you
the story of me having a,

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:28.979
bringing a guitar to graduation.

00:22:28.979 --> 00:22:29.812
(chuckles)

00:22:29.812 --> 00:22:31.470
So you can imagine every now and then

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:33.310
I wasn't probably the easiest student

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:34.990
to have in your classroom.

00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:39.203
And so one of the things
I wish I did was also,

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:42.174
for even some of the teachers

00:22:42.174 --> 00:22:46.806
that I felt weren't
aligned with my worldview,

00:22:46.806 --> 00:22:49.760
I wish that I had reached out to them more

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:51.170
and figured out how I could support them,

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:55.050
and also tell them how much,
what they did meant to me

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:57.177
and to our peers and how
much we grew from it.

00:22:57.177 --> 00:22:58.877
I have to say, even some of those,

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:01.880
you always have your favorites

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:03.017
and then you have those
teachers and you're like,

00:23:03.017 --> 00:23:04.170
"Oh, they're so hard,

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:05.800
and they're just trying
to stick it to us."

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:08.796
But I gotta tell you when
you're 40, 42, 43 years old,

00:23:08.796 --> 00:23:10.540
(chuckles) like myself, I
get together with friends.

00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:12.782
I was like, "That way we
learned a lot in that class."

00:23:12.782 --> 00:23:13.615
(chuckles)

00:23:13.615 --> 00:23:16.571
And so and so teacher
really, by being hard on us,

00:23:16.571 --> 00:23:19.980
prepared us, like prepared us for life.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:24.980
So those are some of the
things that I wish I had known.

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:26.635
And to just know that it all works out,

00:23:26.635 --> 00:23:28.220
it really all works out,

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:31.290
that everything that you
stress about in the moment,

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:34.960
usually a week later, a month
later, for sure, a year later,

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:36.950
you won't even remember what the thing was

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:38.680
that you were worried about.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:40.148
And I still remind myself that

00:23:40.148 --> 00:23:42.040
'cause every now and
then I do have something

00:23:42.040 --> 00:23:42.873
where I wake up in the middle of the night

00:23:42.873 --> 00:23:44.930
and I'm like, "Oh, why is that happening?

00:23:44.930 --> 00:23:47.600
And why ... And then I remind, Sal,

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:50.436
you've gone through
this like 500 times now,

00:23:50.436 --> 00:23:51.985
it's not gonna matter.

00:23:51.985 --> 00:23:52.818
(laughs)

00:23:52.818 --> 00:23:54.450
It's not gonna matter in the long run,

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:56.890
just do what you can, be an open person,

00:23:56.890 --> 00:23:58.460
be an optimistic person

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:01.360
and the universe will
hopefully give you back

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.080
some of that optimism
and openness as well,

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:05.680
and things will resolve themselves.

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:07.000
So those are some of the things.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:08.890
So some of the stress was unnecessary

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:10.880
and some of the anxiety and
some of the insecurities,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:14.420
in fact, all of it was
(laughs) unnecessary.

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:15.713
I wish I had meditated.

00:24:15.713 --> 00:24:18.620
I picked that up about two years ago.

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:20.338
I wish I had started
even 10 minutes a day,

00:24:20.338 --> 00:24:23.091
because then all of the stuff
that I just talked about

00:24:23.091 --> 00:24:26.672
it would have just, it's
like Rain-X for your soul,

00:24:26.672 --> 00:24:29.950
(laughs) things just beat up and fall off,

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:33.273
they don't absorb
(chuckles) into your mind.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:39.000
From Instagram, Louisa Montavonnie asks,

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:41.580
Hi Sal, what are tips to decide

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:43.663
what you want to study in university?

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:50.420
So my advice is don't steer
type yourself too much.

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:52.390
I think it's very easy for us,

00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:53.800
sometimes we get little positive feedback

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:55.346
from one teacher or another
in one subject or another

00:24:55.346 --> 00:24:58.080
saying, "Oh, I'm a writer
or I'm a programmer,

00:24:58.080 --> 00:24:59.350
or I'm a math person."

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.110
And they say, "I'm gonna to be a writer,

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:02.420
or I'm gonna be an art historian"

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:03.270
Because of that feedback.

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:04.280
And that might be the case,

00:25:04.280 --> 00:25:06.520
and those might all be great fields.

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:09.220
But I think it depends
where you go to school,

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:12.040
but in the U.S. there's a lot
of opportunity to try things,

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:13.430
especially your freshman year.

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:17.480
So I would take courses
in a pretty very thing,

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:19.400
I would ... The other thing I wish I knew

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:21.220
is a lot of professors don't mind it.

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:23.110
I used to think, Hey, there's
200 kids in this room.

00:25:23.110 --> 00:25:25.760
The professor has no
interest talking to me,

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:28.349
but I wish I had at least tried.

00:25:28.349 --> 00:25:30.960
Sometimes I did talk to the
professor in smaller classes,

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:32.527
but in those large
classes, I was always like,

00:25:32.527 --> 00:25:34.930
"Oh, I'm just gonna go do
my thing, they don't ...

00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:36.230
But oftentimes you can talk to them,

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:38.570
especially if you're genuinely interested.

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:40.287
There's some students who
are not genuinely interested

00:25:40.287 --> 00:25:43.034
and they're just trying to
suck up to the professor

00:25:43.034 --> 00:25:44.913
(laughs) to maybe get an A,

00:25:44.913 --> 00:25:47.430
but there are also students
are genuinely interested.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:50.480
And if you are genuinely
interested in the course

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:51.750
and the subject matter,

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:54.970
the professor will probably
want to talk to you

00:25:54.970 --> 00:25:56.730
and might even have an internship for you

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:58.830
or have advice about for you.

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:00.970
And so I would do that.

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:02.230
I would talk to as many people,

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:04.410
I would talk to as many
upperclassmen as you can find,

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:07.090
ask them as many questions,
especially the upperclassmen,

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:08.980
the seniors who are getting jobs,

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.380
ask sometimes a little
bit sensitive questions,

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:12.219
how much are you getting paid?

00:26:12.219 --> 00:26:13.052
(chuckles)

00:26:13.052 --> 00:26:13.890
What does the job offer?

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:17.010
What does the job market
look like in that major?

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:19.272
And then if you can, find
to get a hold of alarms,

00:26:19.272 --> 00:26:21.673
people who graduated the last few years,

00:26:22.764 --> 00:26:24.529
because sometimes the work
that you do in the major

00:26:24.529 --> 00:26:28.063
is different than the work
that you do in the job.

00:26:29.396 --> 00:26:32.287
And so talk to the alarms
who majored in X and say,

00:26:32.287 --> 00:26:35.180
"Okay, you got a job,
don't hurt to ask him,

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:36.467
how much money do you get paid?

00:26:36.467 --> 00:26:38.610
"How hard do you have to work?"

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:39.867
All of that, but then ask,

00:26:39.867 --> 00:26:41.310
"What does the actual work like?

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:42.670
What surprised you about it?

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:44.930
What surprise you about that work?"

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:47.860
And you'll learn a lot.

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:49.910
I have to say if there's one thing that,

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:51.410
and I didn't do it well early on,

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:55.010
but by my junior and
senior year and in college,

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:58.350
and for sure, my second
year in business school,

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:00.678
that's what I did, (chuckles)
I was very shameless.

00:27:00.678 --> 00:27:05.010
I remember I really enjoyed
a capital markets class,

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:07.520
which is all about the stock
market and the bond market

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:09.920
and how money moves through the world.

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:12.170
And it was a pretty math, heavy class.

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:17.170
And in business school, I went at HBS,

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:19.373
the math,

00:27:19.373 --> 00:27:21.010
a lot of students didn't
go super deep in the math.

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:23.500
And so I oftentimes was
the closer, so to speak,

00:27:23.500 --> 00:27:24.637
where the professor would say,

00:27:24.637 --> 00:27:26.710
"Sal, how do we solve this equation?"

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:29.940
And so I had a good relationship
with that professor,

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:32.057
his name was George Taco, and I said,

00:27:32.057 --> 00:27:34.130
"George, I love this class.

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:38.140
What can I do in life
that is like this class?"

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:40.697
And he said, "Well, you should
go work at a hedge fund."

00:27:40.697 --> 00:27:42.870
And I said, "That sounds awesome.

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:44.068
What is a hedge fund?"

00:27:44.068 --> 00:27:44.901
(chuckles)

00:27:44.901 --> 00:27:47.050
And then he explained,
"It's like a mutual fund,

00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:49.510
but they can do more exotic things,

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:52.140
they have more flexibility
in how they invest,

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:53.250
but they're regulated differently."

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:54.820
So I was like, "Okay, that's interesting."

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:56.870
And then I looked at all my classmates

00:27:56.870 --> 00:27:58.240
who had worked in finance,

00:27:58.240 --> 00:27:59.220
and then I started going to that.

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:00.530
I was like, "What's a hedge fund?

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:01.363
How much do you get paid?

00:28:01.363 --> 00:28:02.850
What's the lifestyle like?"

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:06.000
And then I was like, "Oh, this
sounds good at least to me. "

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:09.330
And then I started going
through the job search process.

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:10.390
So that wasn't picking the major,

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:11.840
but I was picking the career.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:13.960
But the same process is
true on the major side.

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:16.070
Just ask a ton of questions,

00:28:16.070 --> 00:28:17.600
don't be afraid to ask about money.

00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:20.830
Sometimes on university
campuses money can be viewed

00:28:20.830 --> 00:28:23.450
as a little bit of a ghost
so, "Oh, we're academics.

00:28:23.450 --> 00:28:25.222
How dare you talk about career and money?"

00:28:25.222 --> 00:28:26.055
(chuckles)

00:28:26.055 --> 00:28:27.816
No, talk about money (chuckles)

00:28:27.816 --> 00:28:29.520
Money for sure isn't everything in life,

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:33.580
but you need to be able
to put food on the table,

00:28:33.580 --> 00:28:35.490
support yourself, pay off your debt.

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:38.000
If you're not able to
do that kind of stuff,

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.440
you're not gonna be able to
do the high-minded stuff.

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:42.750
So I'm not saying you have
to become a millionaire

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:45.190
or even have to become a fluent,

00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:47.230
but it's super important to think about,

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:49.840
am I picking a major that
I have a decent chance

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:53.810
of getting a job that I
enjoy, not just tolerate,

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:55.930
but I can enjoy and I can pay off my debt,

00:28:55.930 --> 00:28:59.450
and have a reasonable chance
of saving for a down payment

00:28:59.450 --> 00:29:03.670
on a house, and being
able to go on vacations,

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:05.240
and if I choose to have a family,

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:07.390
to send my kids to college in the future.

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:10.980
So I don't think it's ever
too early to think about that.

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:14.760
All right, so these are great,

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:16.850
maybe we'll do another one of these,

00:29:16.850 --> 00:29:18.130
whenever we don't have a guest.

00:29:18.130 --> 00:29:20.450
Maybe this will be graduation season

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:22.380
'cause I don't know if y'all,

00:29:22.380 --> 00:29:24.592
I'm enjoying playing uncle Sal right now.

00:29:24.592 --> 00:29:27.476
(chuckles)

00:29:27.476 --> 00:29:29.980
So this is ...

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:33.433
So YouTube, first guy, Gomez asks,

00:29:33.433 --> 00:29:35.940
what would you have done differently

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:38.453
if you could start college over again?

00:29:39.980 --> 00:29:41.220
It's the same, and I'll finish on this

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.810
'cause I just realized
I think I do have a call

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:47.201
that I can't reschedule (chuckles)
starting in a minute ago.

00:29:47.201 --> 00:29:50.230
If I could start college over again,

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:51.610
it's the same advice that I said,

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:53.350
I would be that much more open.

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:57.180
I would be, if someone has helped me,

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:59.800
if they mean something to me,
I would have told them that,

00:29:59.800 --> 00:30:02.350
I would have stepped out of my
comfort zone that much more.

00:30:02.350 --> 00:30:03.800
And I did step out of my comfort zone.

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:06.610
When I was, my freshman year in college,

00:30:06.610 --> 00:30:09.160
I intentionally joined
the improv comedy troupe

00:30:09.160 --> 00:30:09.993
because I was like,

00:30:09.993 --> 00:30:11.560
"There's nothing like stepping
out of your comfort zone,

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:12.850
like improv comedy (chuckles)

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:15.610
And we had to travel around
from school to school

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:18.350
and people would shout out
a noun, a verb, a setting,

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:20.840
and context and you'd have to act it out

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:22.991
and try to be somewhat humorous.

00:30:22.991 --> 00:30:23.840
(laughs)

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:25.920
So there's nothing like improv comedy

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:28.210
that will teach you to be
out of your comfort zone.

00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:29.540
But I wish I would have done more of that.

00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:33.500
And I would have been more
open with my thoughts,

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:35.090
my emotions, my insecurities,

00:30:35.090 --> 00:30:36.530
because that's the type of thing

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:39.600
that forms the
connections-deeper friendships.

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:42.570
And I came out of college
with great friendships,

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:44.040
and I feel very fortunate front.

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:45.477
I met my wife in college ,

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:49.290
but I think there could
have been even more.

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:52.450
And even to this day, once a
month I probably have a dream

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:54.490
about college, where I'm in
one of these circumstances,

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:56.010
a lot of it's like the end of the term

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:57.337
and I forgot to drop a class and I'm like,

00:30:57.337 --> 00:30:58.970
"Oh my God."

00:30:58.970 --> 00:30:59.810
Whatever.

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:03.220
But anyway, thanks everyone
for being part of this.

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:07.100
I think this worked out doing
the Instagram and the Facebook

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:08.400
and YouTube at the same time.

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:11.747
And I just wanna thank
everyone for joining this,

00:31:11.747 --> 00:31:13.963
And I'm gonna go jump onto that call now.

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:22.890
See, how do I turn this?

